// Part I — Getting started. Declared mode: tutorial.
page gs_install {
  sites = [:manual]
  title = "Install"

  chapter_header "Install" {
    kicker       = "Getting started · tutorial"
    reading_time = "4 min read"
    updated      = "2026-09-02"
    version      = "config-weave 0.1.0"
  }

  p <<'PROSE'
config-weave is one statically linked executable per platform. In this
tutorial you download it, put it on your `PATH` and confirm it runs. There is
nothing else to install on any machine that runs it: no interpreter, no
agent and no shared libraries.
PROSE

  h2 "Before you start"

  list {
    li "A Linux x86_64 or Windows x86_64 machine you can write to."
    li "A terminal. The commands below use a POSIX shell on Linux and PowerShell on Windows."
    li "Building from source needs a Rust toolchain and network access to fetch the dependencies. Cross-building the release artifacts also needs `cross` and a container runtime."
  }

  h2 "Download a release"

  p <<'PROSE'
Every release on the
[GitHub releases page](https://github.com/Configweave/config-weave/releases)
carries three files: a Linux binary, a Windows binary and a checksums file.
PROSE

  table {
    rows:
      | "File" | "Target" |
      | "`config-weave-linux-x86_64`" | "Linux, `x86_64-unknown-linux-musl`, fully static" |
      | "`config-weave-windows-x86_64.exe`" | "Windows, `x86_64-pc-windows-gnu`, static against the mingw runtime" |
      | "`SHA256SUMS`" | "SHA-256 checksums of both binaries" |
  }

  h3 "Linux"

  p <<'PROSE'
Download the Linux binary and the checksums file into one directory, verify
the checksum, then move the binary to a directory on your `PATH` under the
name `config-weave`.
PROSE

  code sh {
    source = <<'SRC'
sha256sum --check --ignore-missing SHA256SUMS
chmod +x config-weave-linux-x86_64
mkdir -p ~/.local/bin
mv config-weave-linux-x86_64 ~/.local/bin/config-weave
SRC
  }

  p <<'PROSE'
The checksum command prints `config-weave-linux-x86_64: OK`. If `~/.local/bin`
is not on your `PATH`, add it in your shell profile or choose a directory that
is, such as `/usr/local/bin`.
PROSE

  h3 "Windows"

  p <<'PROSE'
Download the Windows binary and the checksums file, verify the hash, then
place the binary in a directory on your `PATH` under the name
`config-weave.exe`. The commands below create a directory under your profile
and add it to the user `PATH`.
PROSE

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
Get-FileHash .\config-weave-windows-x86_64.exe -Algorithm SHA256
Select-String config-weave-windows-x86_64.exe .\SHA256SUMS
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\config-weave"
Move-Item .\config-weave-windows-x86_64.exe "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\config-weave\config-weave.exe"
[Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable("Path", $env:Path + ";$env:LOCALAPPDATA\config-weave", "User")
SRC
  }

  p <<'PROSE'
Compare the hash the first command prints with the line the second command
finds. They match when the download is intact. Open a new terminal so the
updated `PATH` takes effect.
PROSE

  h2 "Verify the install"

  p "Run the version command from any directory."

  code console {
    source = <<'SRC'
$ config-weave version
config-weave 0.1.0
SRC
  }

  callout "You now have config-weave on your PATH" {
    kind = :success
    body = "The command prints the version and exits 0. Every other command in this manual runs from this binary."
  }

  h2 "Build from source instead"

  p <<'PROSE'
If you would rather build the binary yourself, clone the repository and build
with cargo. The build fetches the WCL and wscript crates from GitHub, so it
needs network access the first time.
PROSE

  code sh {
    source = <<'SRC'
git clone https://github.com/Configweave/config-weave.git
cd config-weave
cargo build --release
./target/release/config-weave version
SRC
  }

  p <<'PROSE'
This produces a binary for your own platform. The repository also ships a
`justfile` with the release recipes. `just build` makes a debug build.
`just release` cross-builds both release binaries and the checksums file into
`dist/`, and `just install` copies those into `~/.local/share/config-weave/bin`
and links the Linux binary as `~/.local/bin/config-weave`. Both recipes need
`cross` and a container runtime, because the two targets build inside
containers.
PROSE

  h2 "Next steps"

  p <<'PROSE'
You have a working binary. In [Your first playbook](gs_first_playbook) you
scaffold a playbook, validate it, and take it through check and apply. The
full list of subcommands and global options is in [config-weave](cmd_root).
PROSE
}
